# Data Monitoring Tool - Feature List

## Dataset profiling and quality

- Profile datasets with row and column counts, data types, memory usage, and a data quality score.
- Inspect previews, descriptive statistics, null distributions, correlations, cardinality, and memory use.
- Detect exact duplicates and near-duplicates, including scalable candidate generation for larger datasets.
- Validate expected columns and data types, identify likely renamed columns, and check data freshness.
- Apply configurable column-level business rules and review failed rows.
- Detect IQR outliers and Isolation Forest anomalies.
- Generate data-quality recommendations and summaries; OpenAI-backed summaries are optional.

## Drift, trends, and forecasting

- Compare baseline and current datasets for schema, missing values, duplicates, quality, and feature drift.
- Measure numeric and categorical drift with KS and PSI-based analysis, severity scores, and visualizations.
- Analyze time-series trends and forecast metrics or data health.
- Use linear and ARIMA forecasting; Prophet and TensorFlow-backed LSTM are optional methods with fallback reporting.

## Machine learning and explainability

- Compare candidate classification or regression models and select a best-performing model for a chosen metric.
- Evaluate models on a holdout set with task-appropriate metrics and feature importance.
- Generate per-row explanations with optional SHAP support.
- Run K-means clustering, Isolation Forest-based fraud-pattern screening, predictive-maintenance modeling, and item recommendations.

## Data sources and workflows

- Upload CSV, Excel, JSON, Parquet, TSV, and text files within configured size and dataset limits.
- Load data from local files, SQL databases, PostgreSQL, MySQL, MongoDB, S3, Dropbox, Google Drive, and allowlisted HTTPS JSON APIs when provider configuration is available.
- Check source capabilities and analyze multiple sources through the API.
- Run configurable monitoring workflows for quality, drift, forecasting, and explanations, with tool traces and evidence-based findings.
- Ask questions about a selected dataset through the monitoring assistant.

## Monitoring, history, and reports

- Persist datasets, monitoring results, alert rules and history, configuration, and audit records in SQLite.
- Create immutable, owner-scoped dataset versions; inspect lineage and pipeline events.
- Compare saved datasets or versions and retain comparison results in monitoring history.
- Schedule recurring checks against saved datasets or refreshable sources; source polls create immutable dataset versions and run the selected checks. Pause or resume schedules, edit intervals, and review run status.
- Retry failed scheduled jobs with bounded concurrency and backoff.
- Generate PDF, Excel, and PowerPoint reports, including reports after scheduled runs.
- Configure alert rules and deliver notifications through email, SMS, WhatsApp, Slack, and Microsoft Teams.

## API, dashboard, and access

- Use the FastAPI service and interactive API documentation for authenticated monitoring and dataset operations.
- Explore quality, anomalies, drift, trends and forecasts, explanations, runs, schedules, reports, alerts, comparisons, lineage, and history in the Streamlit dashboards.
- Use the voice assistant for typed questions or browser speech input, with spoken answers when browser support is available.
- Set per-account dashboard language (English, Spanish, French, or Hindi), theme, and overview metric widgets; the dashboard adapts to narrow screens.
- Authenticate with bearer tokens and use admin, analyst, or viewer roles with owner-scoped dataset and result access.
- Protect stored datasets and results with encryption at rest; use password hashing, request-size limits, rate limiting, and admin audit access.
- Check service health and readiness, inspect request metrics, and deploy the API with a separate schedule worker using Docker Compose.

## Availability notes

- Prophet, TensorFlow/LSTM, SHAP, OpenAI summaries, and external source providers require their optional packages or credentials.
- Scheduled monitoring is periodic (minimum interval: 60 seconds); it is not a streaming real-time ingestion system.
- Voice input and speech output depend on browser Web Speech API support and permissions.
